## Null Safety

### 1. Nullable Types

```kotlin
val name: String = "Kotlin"        // Non-null
// name = null                      // ERROR

val nullable: String? = null       // Nullable
nullable = "Value"                 // OK

// Type of nullable is String?
// Type of non-null is String
```

### 2. Safe Operators

**Safe Call (?.):**
```kotlin
val text: String? = "Hello"
val length = text?.length         // 5 if text is not null, null otherwise

val nullText: String? = null
val nullLength = nullText?.length // null
println(nullLength)               // null (no error)
```

**Elvis Operator (?:):**
```kotlin
val name: String? = null
val displayName = name ?: "Guest"  // Uses default if null

val length = text?.length ?: 0     // 0 if text is null
```

**Not-null Assertion (!!):**
```kotlin
val text: String? = "Kotlin"
val length = text!!.length         // Throws if null

// Use only when CERTAIN it's not null
val list: List<String>? = emptyList()
val size = list!!.size             // Safe here, but could crash

// Better: use safe operators
val size2 = list?.size ?: 0        // Safer approach
```

### 3. Type Checking & Casting

```kotlin
val obj: Any = "Hello"

// Smart cast after checking type
if (obj is String) {
    println(obj.length)            // obj is automatically String here
}

// Safe cast
val str = obj as? String           // null if not String
if (str != null) {
    println(str.length)
}

// Explicit cast (unsafe)
val str2 = obj as String           // Throws if not String
```

