### 7. Sealed Classes & Type-Safe Enums

**Interview Question:** "What are sealed classes and why would you use them instead of regular enums?"

**Theory:**
**Sealed Classes** restrict inheritance to predetermined subclasses (known at compile-time). Benefits:
- Type-safe when combined with `when`
- Can have associated data (unlike enums)
- Exhaustive `when` statements
- Better than polymorphism for restricted hierarchies

**Answer:**
```
Sealed classes restrict which classes can inherit.
All subclasses are known at compile-time.

When combined with 'when' expression:
- Compiler ensures ALL cases are covered
- No 'else' needed if all cases handled
- Type-safe pattern matching

Better than:
1. Enums - if you need data with variants
2. Open classes - less safe, more error-prone

Used for: Results, States, Events, ADTs
```

**Examples:**

```kotlin
// Sealed class for type-safe results
sealed class Result<T> {
    data class Success<T>(val data: T) : Result<T>()
    data class Error<T>(val exception: Exception) : Result<T>()
    class Loading<T> : Result<T>()
}

// Exhaustive when (no else needed!)
fun <T> handleResult(result: Result<T>) {
    when (result) {
        is Result.Success -> println("Success: ${result.data}")
        is Result.Error -> println("Error: ${result.exception.message}")
        is Result.Loading -> println("Loading...")
    }
    // Compiler ensures all cases covered
}

// Sealed class for state management
sealed class UiState {
    object Idle : UiState()
    object Loading : UiState()
    data class Success(val data: String) : UiState()
    data class Error(val message: String) : UiState()
}

fun render(state: UiState) {
    when (state) {
        UiState.Idle -> showIdleUI()
        UiState.Loading -> showLoadingSpinner()
        is UiState.Success -> showData(state.data)
        is UiState.Error -> showError(state.message)
    }
}

// Sealed class for events
sealed class UserEvent {
    data class UserCreated(val id: Int, val name: String) : UserEvent()
    data class UserDeleted(val id: Int) : UserEvent()
    data class UserUpdated(val id: Int, val changes: Map<String, Any>) : UserEvent()
    object UserListFetched : UserEvent()
}

fun processEvent(event: UserEvent) {
    when (event) {
        is UserEvent.UserCreated -> addToDatabase(event.id, event.name)
        is UserEvent.UserDeleted -> deleteFromDatabase(event.id)
        is UserEvent.UserUpdated -> updateInDatabase(event.id, event.changes)
        UserEvent.UserListFetched -> refreshUI()
    }
}

// Compare with enum (less flexible)
enum class Status {
    IDLE, LOADING, SUCCESS, ERROR
}
// Can't attach data like Success(data) or Error(exception)

// Sealed class allows data + exhaustiveness
// Best of both worlds!
```

**Interview Tip:**
- "Sealed classes are like enums but can hold data"
- "When combined with when expression, they provide exhaustiveness checking"
- "Use for Results, States, Events - anywhere you have fixed alternatives"
- "Compiler prevents missing cases"

