## Advanced Features

### 12. Extension Functions & Best Practices

**Interview Question:** "What are extension functions and how do they improve code?"

**Theory:**
Extension functions add methods to existing classes without inheritance:
- Extend functionality of classes you don't own
- Improve readability and code organization
- Enable DSL creation
- No runtime overhead (compiled to static methods)

**Answer:**
```
Extension functions add methods to classes 
without inheritance or modification.

fun String.isPhoneNumber(): Boolean {
    return this.matches(Regex("\\d{10}"))
}

"1234567890".isPhoneNumber()  // true

Benefits:
1. Extend classes you don't own (String, List, etc.)
2. Organize related functionality
3. Enable DSLs
4. No runtime cost

Compiled to static method:
StringUtility.isPhoneNumber("1234567890")
```

**Examples:**

```kotlin
// Extend String
fun String.isEmail(): Boolean {
    return this.contains("@") && this.contains(".")
}

"alice@example.com".isEmail()  // true
"invalid".isEmail()             // false

// Extend List
fun <T> List<T>.second(): T? {
    return if (size > 1) this[1] else null
}

listOf(1, 2, 3).second()  // 2

// Extend with parameters
fun String.repeat(times: Int): String {
    return this.repeat(times)
}

"Ha".repeat(3)  // "HaHaHa"

// Extend with receiver
infix fun String.toUpperWith(other: String): String {
    return "${this.uppercase()} $other"
}

"hello" toUpperWith "world"  // "HELLO world"

// Practical extensions
fun <T> List<T>.printEach() {
    forEach { println(it) }
}

listOf("a", "b", "c").printEach()

// Conditional extension
fun String?.isNullOrBlank(): Boolean {
    return this == null || this.isBlank()
}

val empty: String? = null
empty.isNullOrBlank()  // true

// Extension properties
val String.lastChar: Char
    get() = this[this.length - 1]

"Kotlin".lastChar  // 'n'

// Scope extensions using 'receiver'
class Html {
    fun body(init: Body.() -> Unit) = Body().apply(init)
}

class Body {
    fun p(text: String) = println(text)
}

// Usage - HTML DSL
html {
    body {
        p("Hello")
        p("World")
    }
}

// Practical: JSON builder
class JsonBuilder {
    private val properties = mutableMapOf<String, Any>()
    
    infix fun String.to(value: Any) {
        properties[this] = value
    }
    
    fun build() = properties
}

val json = JsonBuilder().apply {
    "name" to "Alice"
    "age" to 25
}.build()

// Best practices
// ✓ GOOD: Clear, specific purpose
fun String.toTitleCase(): String = this.split(" ")
    .map { it.replaceFirstChar { c -> c.uppercase() } }
    .joinToString(" ")

// ✗ BAD: Too generic, confusing
fun String.process(): String = /* ? */

// ✗ BAD: Too many extensions on same class
fun String.doThis() {}
fun String.doThat() {}
fun String.doAnother() {}
```

**Interview Tip:**
- "Extension functions extend classes without inheritance"
- "Use for utility functions on existing classes"
- "Common in Kotlin stdlib: String.toInt(), List.filter(), etc."
- "No runtime cost - compiled to static methods"

