## Null Safety & Type System

### 8. Nullable Types & Safe Operators

**Interview Question:** "How does Kotlin's null safety system work and what is the Elvis operator?"

**Theory:**
Kotlin distinguishes between nullable (`Type?`) and non-nullable (`Type`) types at compile-time:
- Non-nullable by default - must check before use
- Nullable explicitly marked - can use safe operators
- Three operators: `?.` (safe call), `?:` (Elvis), `!!` (not-null assertion)

**Answer:**
```
Kotlin types are non-nullable by default:
val name: String = "Alice"     // Never null
// name = null                 // ERROR at compile time

Nullable types explicitly marked:
val name: String? = "Alice"    // Can be null
name = null                     // OK

Safe operators prevent NPE:
?. - Safe call: returns null if receiver is null
?: - Elvis: provides default value
!! - Not-null assertion: crashes if null (avoid!)

This prevents NullPointerException at compile-time!
```

**Examples:**

```kotlin
// Non-nullable type
val name: String = "Alice"
// name = null                   // ERROR: Type mismatch

// Nullable type
val nickname: String? = null    // OK
val nickname2: String? = "Bob"  // OK

// Safe call operator ?.
val text: String? = "Hello"
val length = text?.length       // 5 if not null, null otherwise

val nullText: String? = null
val nullLength = nullText?.length  // null (no error!)

// Chaining safe calls
val user: User? = getUser()
val email = user?.profile?.email   // null if any step is null

// Elvis operator ?: provides default
val name: String? = null
val displayName = name ?: "Guest"  // "Guest"

val age: Int? = 25
val ageDisplay = age?.toString() ?: "Unknown"  // "25"

// Combination
val text: String? = getText()
val result = text?.uppercase() ?: "EMPTY"

// Not-null assertion !! (use sparingly!)
val name: String? = "Alice"
val length = name!!.length         // 5

val nullName: String? = null
val nullLength = nullName!!.length // CRASH! Throws NPE

// Better: Safe operators instead
val safeName: String? = null
val safeLength = safeName?.length ?: 0  // 0 (no crash)

// Let scope - execute only if not null
val user: User? = getUser()
user?.let { 
    println("User: ${it.name}")  // Only if user is not null
    sendNotification(it)
}

// If-let pattern
if (user != null) {
    println("User: ${user.name}")
    // user is smart-cast to non-null
}

// Elvis with multiple options
val result = option1 ?: option2 ?: option3 ?: "default"

// Practical: Safe navigation in chain
val message = user?.profile?.notificationSettings?.enabled
    ?.let { if (it) "Notifications on" else "Off" }
    ?: "No settings"
```

**Interview Tip:**
- "Kotlin prevents null pointer exceptions at compile time"
- "Use safe operators (?.) and Elvis (?:) instead of null checks"
- "Avoid !! assertions - they defeat the purpose"
- "Safe call chains are safe: null at any step makes whole chain null"

